{# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── the app shell
   `fragment` is set by `app/lib/http.js` (see `isFragment`): when htmx makes a boosted navigation
   request the server renders ONLY the body block below — no doctype, head, topbar, nav or script
   tags — and htmx swaps it into `#app-main`. The shell is therefore rendered once and never
   re-rendered on a nav click. A browser with no htmx (or a direct GET) gets the whole document,
   unchanged. `{% block body %}` stays OUTSIDE the guards on purpose: it is the one thing both modes
   emit, and a block inside `{% if %}` would be conditional for the child templates that inherit it. #}
{% if not fragment %}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }} · {{ appName }}</title>
{# The data version the client's IndexedDB reference cache syncs against. Empty means "sync now".
   It carries an id so a fragment can update it out-of-band (see the OOB meta in the body below and
   `__AA_REFDB__.reload()`), keeping the cache honest across a boosted nav without a full load. #}
<meta id="aa-refdb-version" name="aa-refdb-version" content="{{ refdbVersion }}">
{# The signed-in identity, so the client page cache (`pagecache.js`) never serves one user's cached
    fragments to another on a shared browser. The shell is per-identity; this is the same key material. #}
<meta id="aa-user" name="aa-user" content="{{ user.email if user else '' }}">
<link rel="icon" href="/static/favicon.svg">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/css/theme.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/css/app.css">
</head>
{# `paper` selects the print sheet: a5 for an invoice or a voucher, a4 for a report. See app.css. #}
{# htmx does not send a header the guest host passes through (`HX-Request` is not in the host's
   header allowlist). It does inherit `hx-headers`, so every htmx request from this page carries
   `X-Requested-With`, which the host DOES expose — see `app/lib/http.js` `isFragment`. Inherited from
   `<body>` so the rail links, the topbar brand and any future boosted element all send it. #}
<body{% if paper %} data-paper="{{ paper }}"{% endif %} hx-headers='{"X-Requested-With":"XMLHttpRequest"}' hx-indicator="#aa-loading">
{# The loading spinner. htmx adds its request class to the element named by `hx-indicator` for the
   duration of every htmx request, and `hx-indicator` is inherited from this `<body>` — so one element
   covers navigation, filters, sorts and writes. It lives OUTSIDE `#app-main` (in the shell, rendered
   once) so a swap never removes it. A cache hit is served by `pagecache.js` without an htmx request, so
   it shows nothing — which is correct, because it is instant. The show is delayed ~150 ms in CSS, so a
   fast request does not flash it. #}
<div id="aa-loading" class="aa-loading" role="status" aria-live="polite">
  <span class="aa-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="aa-loading-text">Loading…</span>
</div>
<div class="appshell">
{% if user %}
{% include 'partials/topbar.html.jinja' %}
<div class="appbody">
{% include 'partials/nav.html.jinja' %}
{% endif %}
<main id="app-main" class="main{% if not user %} main-anon{% endif %}">
{% endif %}{# ── end of the full-document shell; a fragment starts here ─────────────────────────────────── #}
{# A fragment carries no <head>, so the browser tab would keep the previous page's title after a
   boosted nav. This hidden marker travels with the fragment and `app.js` copies it onto
   `document.title` on `htmx:afterSwap`. Full-document loads get their title from <head> as before,
   so the marker is emitted only in fragment mode and never changes the full page. #}
{% if fragment %}<div hidden data-aa-title="{{ title }} · {{ appName }}"></div>{% endif %}
{# Out-of-band: the fragment's own data version replaces the head meta, so the client reference cache
   (`refdb.js`) sees a version change after a boosted nav and re-syncs (`__AA_REFDB__.reload()`, driven
   from the `htmx:afterSwap` hook). Without this the meta — which lives in `<head>` and is never
   swapped — would stay at the version the full page loaded with. #}
{% if fragment %}<meta name="aa-refdb-version" content="{{ refdbVersion }}" hx-swap-oob="outerHTML:#aa-refdb-version">{% endif %}
{% include 'partials/flash.html.jinja' %}
{% block body %}{% endblock %}
{% if not fragment %}</main>
{% if user %}</div>{% endif %}
</div>
{# htmx, vendored locally (the deployment is offline — no CDN). Boosted navigation keeps the shell and
   swaps only `#app-main`; without it the `hx-*` attributes are inert and every link behaves normally.
   Loaded first so `app.js`, which listens for `htmx:afterSwap` to re-enhance swapped content, always
   finds it loaded. #}
<script src="/static/js/htmx.min.js" defer></script>
<script src="/static/js/app.js" defer></script>
{# The client page cache: IndexedDB of rendered fragments keyed on (code revision, data version, url),
    served on a boosted nav without a request ([[guest-page-cache]]). Loaded after app.js so the
    `htmx:afterSwap` event it dispatches on a cache hit reaches app.js's re-enhancement listener, which
    was registered first. Additive and fail-safe: a miss is an ordinary htmx request. #}
<script src="/static/js/pagecache.js" defer></script>
{# The print queue: the legacy browser-side queue (localStorage `print-queue`) behind "Add to Print
   Queue" on the invoice sheet and the Queue rail on `/print`. Loaded before the other components so
   the click delegation is registered early; it is progressive enhancement, so absence is safe. #}
<script src="/static/js/print-queue.js" defer></script>
{# Web components. Each is progressive enhancement over markup that already works — a custom element
   that has not been upgraded leaves a normal form control in place. `date-input.js` gives every
   `<aa-date-input>` the legacy Mantine `DateInput` look (text field, no browser calendar glyph) while
   keeping the native `<input type="date">` as the submitted value. Loaded with `defer` so the DOM is
   parsed before upgrade. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/date-input.js" defer></script>
{# `modal.js` gives `<aa-modal>` the legacy Mantine dialog — the centered card, the header with its X,
   the overlay and Escape-to-close. It degrades to the authored children shown inline when it is not
   upgraded, so a dialog's form still works with no JavaScript. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/modal.js" defer></script>
{# `<aa-live-time>` is the ticking header clock. The header's `.header-clock` slot is the element
    itself, so the slot renders nothing until this upgrades. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/live-time.js" defer></script>
{# `<aa-money>` normalises an amount to 2 dp and, in `rate` mode, never reduces a unit price/cost below
    its stored precision — the ledger-rewriting defect of [[minimise-client-side-calculation]]. It also
    gives a rate `step="any"`, which its `step="0.01"` counterpart could not (see components/money.js). #}
<script src="/static/js/components/money.js" defer></script>
{# `<aa-entity-form>` wraps a server-rendered write form: inline required-field messages, an inline
    surface for a bare 4xx/5xx htmx would not swap, and disabled-during-flight. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/entity-form.js" defer></script>
{# `<aa-flash>` gives the write-feedback banner a lifetime — it clears a success, and offers a dismiss
    on an error — without changing the `.flash` markup the server renders. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/flash.js" defer></script>
{# `<aa-invoice-entry>` is the entry screen's container: it consumes the server descriptor and owns the
    item→price fill, which used to live in app.js. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/invoice-entry.js" defer></script>
{# `<aa-invoice-view>` is the keyboard invoice console's controller: the 0/999/000/up state machine and
    focus walk over the server-rendered console. `<aa-item-search>` is the `000` advanced item-search
    dialog it opens. Both are progressive enhancement over forms that already work. #}
<script src="/static/js/components/invoice-view.js" defer></script>
<script src="/static/js/components/item-search.js" defer></script>
{# The keyboard layer is a separate file on purpose: it is an accelerator for markup that already works,
   so it can be reasoned about, tested and dropped without touching the app's own enhancements.
   `?` lists everything it can do on this page. #}
<script src="/static/js/hotkeys.js" defer></script>
{# Per-browser preferences (`localStorage`). Loaded before `autocomplete.js` so a scope-trimmed invoice
   picker is what the combobox is built from; `app.js` re-applies it on an htmx swap for the same
   reason. See `settings.js` and the Settings screen at `/settings`. #}
<script src="/static/js/settings.js" defer></script>
{# The IndexedDB reference cache. Loaded before autocomplete.js so a select it repopulates is seen by
   the control; refdb dispatches `refdb:select` on a select whose options it changed, which the
   control listens for, so order is not load-bearing. Server-rendered options stay in the markup, so
   a page with no JavaScript is unchanged. See [[reference-lists-come-from-indexeddb]]. #}
<script src="/static/js/refdb.js" defer></script>
{# The searchable control for long selects. It keeps the native <select> in the form, so a page with no
   JavaScript is still a working form — this only replaces how the control looks and how it is picked
   from. Loaded after app.js because it reads the selects app.js has already enhanced line editors on. #}
<script src="/static/js/autocomplete.js" defer></script>
{# The "Create …" panel's no-reload path: POST /quick-create, put the row in the refdb cache and
   select it. Falls back to the panel's normal form submit if the cache is absent or the fetch fails,
   so the form path is never lost. Loaded after autocomplete.js because it relies on the control's
   Escape-to-close gesture. See [[reference-lists-come-from-indexeddb]]. #}
<script src="/static/js/refdb-create.js" defer></script>
{# The invoice lookup cache. Loaded LAST so `autocomplete.js` has already built its combobox: this
   populates the picker from IndexedDB and fires `refdb:select`, which the combobox listens for. Loaded
   lazily — it only fetches when the page actually has an invoice picker. See `invoicedb.js`. #}
<script src="/static/js/invoicedb.js" defer></script>
{# Server-side invoice-number search for a picker that opts in with `data-invoice-remote` (the
   console's invoice #). Loaded after autocomplete.js so the combobox it drives already exists; it
   replaces the select's options and fires `refdb:select` to re-read them. See `invoice-remote.js`. #}
<script src="/static/js/invoice-remote.js" defer></script>
{# Supply Summary's picker: "Select all" / "Clear" over the invoice checkboxes (`data-picker-*`). #}
<script src="/static/js/supply-summary.js" defer></script>
</body>
</html>{% endif %}
